---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
import Workspace from '../components/Workspace.astro';
import CTA from '../components/CTA.astro';
---

<Layout>
  <section class="page-intro">
    <div class="container">
      <p class="eyebrow">THE CIVOLYN PLATFORM</p>
      <div class="intro-split">
        <h1>
          One workspace.
          <br />
          <em>A clearer way forward.</em>
        </h1>
        <p class="intro-copy">
          An unanswered question holds up a task. An unfinished task changes the
          report. Your work is connected. CIVOLYN imagines tools that see those
          connections, too.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="intro-rule">
        <span>A unified workspace for work, context and direction.</span>
        <span>FICTIONAL PLATFORM CONCEPT / 01</span>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
  <section class="container">
    <div class="platform-stage">
      <Workspace />
    </div>
    <p class="section-caption">
      <span>A concept workspace, built around the way a team works.</span>
      <span>Illustrative content</span>
    </p>
  </section>
  <section class="section">
    <div class="container platform-principle">
      <div>
        <p class="eyebrow">THE IDEA IS SIMPLE</p>
        <blockquote>
          Give work a shared context.
          <br />
          <em>Keep the next step in sight.</em>
        </blockquote>
      </div>
      <div class="editorial-copy">
        <p>
          In a growing team, the cost of switching tools is often the context
          left behind. A brief lives in one place. Its approval happens
          somewhere else. The numbers arrive after everyone has moved on.
        </p>
        <p>
          CIVOLYN connects three useful perspectives: what needs to happen, what
          the team already knows, and what the activity can tell us. The concept
          brings those perspectives into one calm workspace without making every
          task look the same.
        </p>
        <p>
          It is an exploration of a possible product experience. The previews
          use local example data; they do not connect your tools or run real
          business processes.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
  <section class="section dark-section">
    <div class="container">
      <p class="eyebrow">A CONNECTED DAY, FROM START TO FINISH</p>
      <h2>
        One request.
        <br />
        <em>Three ways to make it clearer.</em>
      </h2>
      <div class="connected-story">
        <article>
          <span class="number">01 / MOVE</span>
          <h3>A new project comes in.</h3>
          <p>
            Flow gives an illustrative intake request a visible path: gather a
            brief, prepare a summary, ask for approval and identify the next
            owner.
          </p>
          <a class="arrow-link" href="/products/flow/">
            Explore Flow <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span>
          </a>
        </article>
        <article>
          <span class="number">02 / UNDERSTAND</span>
          <h3>The right context is close.</h3>
          <p>
            Knowledge gives the team a place to find the onboarding guide and
            review policy. A useful answer stays connected to its original
            reference.
          </p>
          <a class="arrow-link" href="/products/knowledge/">
            Explore Knowledge <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span>
          </a>
        </article>
        <article>
          <span class="number">03 / REFLECT</span>
          <h3>The pattern comes into view.</h3>
          <p>
            Signal presents example completion volumes over time. The team can
            ask what changed, where a queue is growing and what needs a closer
            look.
          </p>
          <a class="arrow-link" href="/products/signal/">
            Explore Signal <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span>
          </a>
        </article>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
  <section class="section">
    <div class="container split-section">
      <div>
        <p class="eyebrow">CONNECTED DOESN’T HAVE TO MEAN COMPLICATED</p>
        <h2>
          A common foundation.
          <br />
          <em>Room for different work.</em>
        </h2>
        <p>
          A small operations team and a project lead may begin in different
          places. The underlying principles stay consistent.
        </p>
        <a class="arrow-link" href="/compare/">
          Compare the three products <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span>
        </a>
      </div>
      <ol class="numbered-list">
        <li>
          <span class="number">01</span>
          <div>
            <h3>Context travels with the task.</h3>
            <p>
              Keep a brief, a relevant reference and a next step near each
              other, so handovers need less explanation.
            </p>
          </div>
        </li>
        <li>
          <span class="number">02</span>
          <div>
            <h3>People stay in the loop.</h3>
            <p>
              A suggestion is a starting point. Review checkpoints make space
              for the judgment that a process cannot supply.
            </p>
          </div>
        </li>
        <li>
          <span class="number">03</span>
          <div>
            <h3>Progress can be understood.</h3>
            <p>
              Clear labels and source references help people interpret what they
              see, including what the examples cannot tell them.
            </p>
          </div>
        </li>
      </ol>
    </div>
  </section>
  <CTA title="Find a little more room in your day." />
</Layout>
